Root cause identified and fixed in the STM32F4 Discovery `miniblink` example.

### Root cause
The LED delay loop counter was declared as a plain non-volatile local:
```c
int i;
```
With optimization enabled, the compiler can optimize the empty wait loop away (or collapse it), causing `gpio_toggle()` to execute effectively at full speed. To the eye, that appears as a solid LED after startup instead of a visible blink.

### Fix applied
File updated:
`firmware/libopencm3-examples/examples/stm32/f4/stm32f4-discovery/miniblink/miniblink.c`

Changes:
- Added `#include <stdint.h>`
- Changed loop counter type and volatility:
```c
volatile uint32_t i;
```

This keeps the delay loop from being optimized out and restores visible blinking behavior.

### Patch summary
```diff
+#include <stdint.h>
...
-int i;
+volatile uint32_t i;
```

This directly addresses the “lights once, then stays on” symptom by restoring deterministic software delay timing in the blink loop.
